WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.114
(cheering and applauding)

00:00:02.114 --> 00:00:02.947
- [Kelly] You're right.

00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:05.365
Like, all that crazy stuff I said,

00:00:05.365 --> 00:00:06.751
and you look like Wonder Woman.

00:00:06.751 --> 00:00:09.768
I'm like, what? You're,
you're like a goddess.

00:00:09.768 --> 00:00:10.829
- Well, thank you, darling.

00:00:10.829 --> 00:00:13.273
- [Kelly] Geez. I was like,
have you ever met Jane?

00:00:13.273 --> 00:00:15.197
- We have never met in person.

00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:16.141
- It is very nice to meet you.

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:16.974
- Yes.

00:00:16.974 --> 00:00:17.807
- I like a tall chick.

00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:18.640
- Yeah.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.473
- How tall are you, dear?

00:00:19.473 --> 00:00:20.306
- Well, short.

00:00:20.306 --> 00:00:21.139
- Well, in the heels, like, 6'4.

00:00:21.139 --> 00:00:22.360
- Oh, but you're a six-footer then?

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.193
- Yeah.

00:00:23.193 --> 00:00:24.088
- I'm a six-footer too.
- There we go.

00:00:24.088 --> 00:00:24.922
- High five on that, sis.
- Hey. Thank you.

00:00:24.922 --> 00:00:26.455
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:26.455 --> 00:00:28.470
Okay, no, okay, no?

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.303
Just me?

00:00:29.303 --> 00:00:30.786
5'3, guys.

00:00:30.786 --> 00:00:32.863
Short people are cool too.

00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:34.046
Well, take us back.

00:00:34.046 --> 00:00:35.649
Did you always have a
passion for marketing?

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:37.473
Did you know this was your, like, path?

00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:38.790
- No, I did not.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.327
My parents are originally
from Ghana in West Africa.

00:00:41.327 --> 00:00:43.979
So being first-generation American,

00:00:43.979 --> 00:00:46.317
they wanted me to only
have one of three careers.

00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:48.662
I could be a doctor, a
lawyer, or an engineer.

00:00:48.662 --> 00:00:49.495
That was it.

00:00:49.495 --> 00:00:50.328
- My God.

00:00:50.328 --> 00:00:51.161
- You know what I mean?

00:00:51.161 --> 00:00:51.994
Marketing was not-
- Bars high.

00:00:51.994 --> 00:00:52.827
- Yeah, exactly.

00:00:52.827 --> 00:00:54.724
So in college, I was pre-med.

00:00:54.724 --> 00:00:56.768
Then I graduated with also a degree

00:00:56.768 --> 00:00:59.338
in African-American History and English.

00:00:59.338 --> 00:01:00.888
I mean, I was just doing all the things.

00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:01.721
- [Kelly] That's cool.

00:01:01.721 --> 00:01:02.554
That's what you're
supposed to do in college.

00:01:02.554 --> 00:01:03.789
- I mean, you just explore, right?

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:04.622
- Yeah.

00:01:04.622 --> 00:01:06.659
- But then I wanted to
take a little bit of time

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:08.317
before going to medical school to try

00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:10.514
and, you know, figure
out what I wanted to do.

00:01:10.514 --> 00:01:12.775
And I thought New York
City is big bad city.

00:01:12.775 --> 00:01:13.608
I want to go, you know,

00:01:13.608 --> 00:01:15.274
check it out and see what's happening.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:20.274
- Yeah, so I went there and
discovered marketing. (laughs)

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.113
- [Kelly] How?

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:21.946
- Yeah.

00:01:21.946 --> 00:01:22.779
- Like what?

00:01:22.779 --> 00:01:23.612
- So here's the thing.

00:01:23.612 --> 00:01:24.445
Now I'm a hustler.

00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:25.278
- Okay.

00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:27.665
- So I went to New York, no money.

00:01:27.665 --> 00:01:29.036
I had one friend there.

00:01:29.036 --> 00:01:31.027
I was sleeping on her couch,

00:01:31.027 --> 00:01:33.085
and I would take jobs at temp agencies

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:35.661
to sort of make ends meet
to feed myself, right?

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:36.494
- Yeah.

00:01:36.494 --> 00:01:39.452
- So literally on the corner
of 125th Street and Broadway,

00:01:39.452 --> 00:01:40.419
there was a phone booth.

00:01:40.419 --> 00:01:42.136
This is how long ago that was, you know.

00:01:42.136 --> 00:01:42.969
- Phone booth.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:44.194
- It wasn't, it wasn't a cell phone.

00:01:44.194 --> 00:01:45.027
- Yeah,

00:01:45.027 --> 00:01:45.860
- It was a phone booth.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.385
And I would call into the temp
agency every single morning.

00:01:48.385 --> 00:01:49.795
I would start calling them at 7:30,

00:01:49.795 --> 00:01:52.300
call 'em every 15 minutes
until I got a job.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.755
And, like, it took, like, six weeks

00:01:54.755 --> 00:01:58.359
for me every single day
calling and then getting a job.

00:01:58.359 --> 00:01:59.459
Finally, it was, like,

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:01.800
the golden day when I got a job

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.195
to be the receptionist at
Spike's advertising agency.

00:02:04.195 --> 00:02:05.056
- Oh, wow.

00:02:05.056 --> 00:02:05.923
- And so I get there,

00:02:05.923 --> 00:02:08.771
and I'm only supposed
to answer the phones.

00:02:08.771 --> 00:02:11.629
Now I see he's carrying around
something under his arm,

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:13.581
coming back and forth in the office.

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:16.438
It happened to be the
script for "Bamboozled".

00:02:16.438 --> 00:02:18.392
And I asked him if I could read it,

00:02:18.392 --> 00:02:20.131
being an African-American studies,

00:02:20.131 --> 00:02:21.912
you know, literature person.

00:02:21.912 --> 00:02:22.745
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:22.745 --> 00:02:24.333
- I thought I could
perhaps give him some tips.

00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:25.166
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:25.166 --> 00:02:25.999
- You know what I mean?

00:02:25.999 --> 00:02:26.832
- I love it. Bold, though.

00:02:26.832 --> 00:02:27.937
- Yeah, and naive.

00:02:27.937 --> 00:02:28.770
- No.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.359
- But it was, but it was perfect

00:02:30.359 --> 00:02:32.109
because it's like, I think today,

00:02:32.109 --> 00:02:33.464
I probably would've hesitated, right?

00:02:33.464 --> 00:02:34.297
I would've been like:

00:02:34.297 --> 00:02:35.130
"Oh, Spike, like, how can you

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.039
even give him any kind of tips at all?"

00:02:37.039 --> 00:02:38.061
But at the time I was like:

00:02:38.061 --> 00:02:39.853
"Oh, yes, I'm gonna do him a favor."

00:02:39.853 --> 00:02:40.686
(claps)

00:02:40.686 --> 00:02:41.848
- So I took, you know what I mean?

00:02:41.848 --> 00:02:43.096
So I took the script, I marked it up,

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:43.929
gave it back to him.

00:02:43.929 --> 00:02:44.888
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:44.888 --> 00:02:45.955
- He looked at me like I would,

00:02:45.955 --> 00:02:47.621
had lost my whole mind

00:02:47.621 --> 00:02:50.093
and, no, but he looked at it and said:

00:02:50.093 --> 00:02:52.062
"Okay, like, actually you
make some good points.

00:02:52.062 --> 00:02:53.655
You should, you should
stay here for a while."

00:02:53.655 --> 00:02:54.627
- [Kelly] Oh, my gosh.

00:02:54.627 --> 00:02:55.460
- That's what got my job.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.749
- But that's what I find incredible.

00:02:56.749 --> 00:02:58.189
But I think things I did too.

00:02:58.189 --> 00:02:59.139
Whenever I was, like, 19,

00:02:59.139 --> 00:03:01.571
I was, like, I moved to a
whole, across the state.

00:03:01.571 --> 00:03:02.936
I lived here, didn't know anybody,

00:03:02.936 --> 00:03:04.184
lived on a mattress, like-

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:05.017
- You know what I'm saying?

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:06.211
- and things to do when you're young,

00:03:06.211 --> 00:03:07.448
I'm like: "I don't know
if I'd do that now."

00:03:07.448 --> 00:03:08.281
- Exactly.

00:03:08.281 --> 00:03:09.795
- It sounds like a real bad situation.

00:03:09.795 --> 00:03:10.883
- Right, you know better now.

00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:11.716
- Yeah, it sounds hard up.

00:03:11.716 --> 00:03:14.600
- Yeah, it's so much better to do it then.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.691
- Yeah. Did you do crazy stuff

00:03:15.691 --> 00:03:16.583
like that when you were young?

00:03:16.583 --> 00:03:17.966
- Yeah, like, flophouses with,

00:03:17.966 --> 00:03:20.101
you know, a bunch of
friends putting on shows,

00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:21.094
and we'd all lived together

00:03:21.094 --> 00:03:22.822
and, you know, horrible little homes.

00:03:22.822 --> 00:03:24.314
(laughing)

00:03:24.314 --> 00:03:25.319
- Good times.

00:03:25.319 --> 00:03:29.031
Well, so then 10 years ago,
everything shifted in your life.

00:03:29.031 --> 00:03:30.001
So explain what happened.

00:03:30.001 --> 00:03:31.825
- Yeah, well, 10 years ago,

00:03:31.825 --> 00:03:33.648
I felt like I was really at the,

00:03:33.648 --> 00:03:35.153
at a high, high in my career.

00:03:35.153 --> 00:03:36.486
I was at PepsiCo.

00:03:36.486 --> 00:03:39.557
I had worked with Beyoncé
on the deal with the NFL-

00:03:39.557 --> 00:03:40.390
- Yeah.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.532
- to put her on the "Super
Bowl Halftime Show".

00:03:41.532 --> 00:03:43.153
I mean, we were like,
everything was popping.

00:03:43.153 --> 00:03:43.986
- Yeah.

00:03:43.986 --> 00:03:47.653
- And I had been married
for about 10 years,

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:52.540
and my husband, Peter,
was diagnosed with cancer.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:53.373
- [Jane] Uh-huh.

00:03:53.373 --> 00:03:54.727
- In May of 2013.

00:03:54.727 --> 00:03:55.792
- [Kelly] Oh, wow.

00:03:55.792 --> 00:03:57.211
- And our daughter was four.

00:03:57.211 --> 00:03:59.569
You know, we were both, like,
doing very well in our career.

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.564
So you don't really think
anything bad is ever gonna happen.

00:04:01.564 --> 00:04:02.397
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:02.397 --> 00:04:04.935
- And by December, he was dead.

00:04:04.935 --> 00:04:06.524
- [Jane] Oh, God.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:06.524 --> 00:04:09.681
- It was a very fast terminal cancer

00:04:09.681 --> 00:04:11.237
that we were not prepared for.

00:04:11.237 --> 00:04:12.252
I certainly wasn't.

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:14.643
And I really was unprepared

00:04:14.643 --> 00:04:17.286
to be a single mom to
try and figure out life.

00:04:17.286 --> 00:04:18.119
- Yeah.

00:04:18.119 --> 00:04:20.719
- You know, by myself, all the things.

00:04:20.719 --> 00:04:23.396
But I also have to be honest
that up until then, you know,

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:24.444
our marriage had been good,

00:04:24.444 --> 00:04:26.192
but then we hit some rocky parts too.

00:04:26.192 --> 00:04:27.025
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:27.025 --> 00:04:27.900
- You know, like many marriages.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.733
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:29.566
- You know, we were separated.

00:04:29.566 --> 00:04:30.399
- I don't what you're speaking of.

00:04:30.399 --> 00:04:32.444
- Right, well, yeah, exactly.

00:04:32.444 --> 00:04:33.277
- Tell me.

00:04:33.277 --> 00:04:34.110
(laughing)

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:34.943
- Yeah, you know, something we-

00:04:34.943 --> 00:04:35.776
- It happens.

00:04:35.776 --> 00:04:36.609
- It happens.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.279
Yeah, you know, we were
separated for a few years.

00:04:38.279 --> 00:04:39.112
- Yeah.

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:41.815
- And never got all
the way to the divorce.

00:04:41.815 --> 00:04:45.775
And then when he was diagnosed
as terminal, we reconciled,

00:04:45.775 --> 00:04:48.551
you know, to get through
the last bits of his life

00:04:48.551 --> 00:04:49.384
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:49.384 --> 00:04:50.217
- [Bozoma] before he died.

00:04:50.217 --> 00:04:52.028
- [Kelly] Also, I bet for
your daughter as well.

00:04:52.028 --> 00:04:53.884
- Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes.

00:04:53.884 --> 00:04:57.766
All the things. I wanted to
be able to have her share,

00:04:57.766 --> 00:04:59.143
you know, the moment with us.

00:04:59.143 --> 00:04:59.976
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:59.976 --> 00:05:01.500
- And I got some really great advice

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.217
from a friend then who said,

00:05:03.217 --> 00:05:04.817
you know, like, tell her the truth.

00:05:04.817 --> 00:05:05.650
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.748
- Like, you know, even though she was four

00:05:06.748 --> 00:05:07.994
because you don't wanna
do that, right, as a mom?

00:05:07.994 --> 00:05:09.713
- Yeah, but, like, what
are you supposed to say?

00:05:09.713 --> 00:05:12.444
- I mean, right, you
use, like, soft words.

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:13.958
But the advice I got, which, by the way,

00:05:13.958 --> 00:05:16.229
I've used up until now, is
that you tell the truth.

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:17.062
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:17.062 --> 00:05:18.215
- You know, use the right words,

00:05:18.215 --> 00:05:20.262
like, if you say, "Oh, Daddy's sick."

00:05:20.262 --> 00:05:21.095
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:21.095 --> 00:05:22.279
- Well, every time,
like, somebody has a cold

00:05:22.279 --> 00:05:24.116
or something else, and you
think: "Oh, they're sick."

00:05:24.116 --> 00:05:26.000
She's gonna think something
terrible's about to happen.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:26.833
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:28.176
- So we use the right words, you know?

00:05:28.176 --> 00:05:29.009
- [Kelly] Absolutely.

00:05:29.009 --> 00:05:30.236
- And when I had to tell
her that he was gone.

00:05:30.236 --> 00:05:31.665
- I said exactly that.

00:05:31.665 --> 00:05:32.679
- Ooh, that's hard.

00:05:32.679 --> 00:05:33.512
- It is.

00:05:33.512 --> 00:05:35.020
- After losing your husband,

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.276
you said you had real
conversations with your daughters.

00:05:37.276 --> 00:05:38.109
- Yeah.

00:05:38.109 --> 00:05:39.132
- So how did that shape you as a mama?

00:05:39.132 --> 00:05:39.965
- Oh, my gosh.

00:05:39.965 --> 00:05:41.340
- [Kelly] Those are hard conversations.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.448
I've had, I think you all,
if you have kids at any,

00:05:43.448 --> 00:05:44.380
my, mine are six and eight.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:45.213
- Mm-hm.

00:05:45.213 --> 00:05:46.778
- They are terrified of death right now.

00:05:46.778 --> 00:05:47.611
- Right.

00:05:47.611 --> 00:05:49.253
- They just, you know,
it's like people have died,

00:05:49.253 --> 00:05:51.163
like, in their lives, and
they, they're terrified of it.

00:05:51.163 --> 00:05:52.934
They think any, anytime
I leave on a plane,

00:05:52.934 --> 00:05:54.087
anytime I do anything, they're like:

00:05:54.087 --> 00:05:55.195
"Are you gonna come back?"

00:05:55.195 --> 00:05:57.127
And I'm like, I, and I always, I'm honest.

00:05:57.127 --> 00:05:59.738
I'm like: "I don't know, but
let's not wish the worst."

00:05:59.738 --> 00:06:00.571
- Like, right?

00:06:00.571 --> 00:06:01.404
Like, I don't know.

00:06:01.404 --> 00:06:02.962
Yeah, I'm hoping I do.

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:04.437
Like, yeah.

00:06:04.437 --> 00:06:05.308
- But I think in the back of our minds,

00:06:05.308 --> 00:06:06.662
we all consider that, right?

00:06:06.662 --> 00:06:07.495
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:07.495 --> 00:06:08.453
- We all consider things,

00:06:08.453 --> 00:06:10.406
and maybe we don't wanna
talk about it exactly,

00:06:10.406 --> 00:06:11.952
which is why I think it's so important

00:06:11.952 --> 00:06:13.436
for us to be that much more-

00:06:13.436 --> 00:06:14.269
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:14.269 --> 00:06:15.580
- brave and courageous about it

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:16.817
'cause you don't know what's gonna happen.

00:06:16.817 --> 00:06:17.650
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:19.526
- And I don't think it's
a morbid thing to do.

00:06:19.526 --> 00:06:21.629
I should think it's very inspiring.

00:06:21.629 --> 00:06:24.817
But the light for us has been
that Lael, who's my daughter.

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:25.788
She's now 13 and a half.

00:06:25.788 --> 00:06:26.801
- It's such a beautiful name.

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:27.634
- Oh, thank you.

00:06:27.634 --> 00:06:28.678
- Yeah, Lael, that's beautiful.

00:06:28.678 --> 00:06:29.511
- Lael.

00:06:29.511 --> 00:06:31.259
And I told her, I was like:

00:06:31.259 --> 00:06:32.675
"Look, you're gonna become my partner

00:06:32.675 --> 00:06:34.555
in this, okay, in raising you."

00:06:34.555 --> 00:06:35.388
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:35.388 --> 00:06:36.796
- And she looked at me like: "Okay."

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:38.502
But I was like: "No, you're
gonna be my partner, and-

00:06:38.502 --> 00:06:39.335
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:39.335 --> 00:06:40.625
- you're gonna, you're gonna tell me

00:06:40.625 --> 00:06:41.489
the things you need.

00:06:41.489 --> 00:06:42.322
- Be honest.

00:06:42.322 --> 00:06:43.155
- And I will tell you the things I need,

00:06:43.155 --> 00:06:44.540
and we're gonna work it out together.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.854
It has given her a voice in our house

00:06:46.854 --> 00:06:49.446
and a voice in our, in our relationship.

00:06:49.446 --> 00:06:51.127
And so I feel that it's a real benefit.

00:06:51.127 --> 00:06:51.960
- It also teaches her how to have

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.106
relationships like that, to be honest.

00:06:54.106 --> 00:06:54.961
- [Bozoma] Yes.

00:06:54.961 --> 00:06:55.983
- What boundaries are created.

00:06:55.983 --> 00:06:56.998
- Yeah, that's beautiful.

00:06:56.998 --> 00:06:57.831
- And what you need.

00:06:57.831 --> 00:06:58.664
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:58.664 --> 00:06:59.497
- What you need.

00:06:59.497 --> 00:07:00.330
- [Kelly] 'Cause sometimes people

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:01.163
aren't good at saying that.

00:07:01.163 --> 00:07:01.996
They're coddled their whole life

00:07:01.996 --> 00:07:02.829
and they aren't taught that.

00:07:02.829 --> 00:07:03.662
And then all of a sudden
you're in college,

00:07:03.662 --> 00:07:05.111
and you're, like, have these
unhealthy relationships,

00:07:05.111 --> 00:07:06.602
and you're, they don't
know how to deal with it.

00:07:06.602 --> 00:07:08.230
- Ooh, I need her to have a voice.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:09.276
- [Jane] Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:07:09.276 --> 00:07:10.204
- [Kelly] Absolutely, absolutely.

00:07:10.204 --> 00:07:11.548
Well, you, I imagine your daughter's

00:07:11.548 --> 00:07:12.699
definitely gonna have a voice.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:14.577
I read your resume. I was like.

00:07:14.577 --> 00:07:17.244
So you speak about being the
only black person in a room.

00:07:17.244 --> 00:07:19.729
So have you seen that change at all or no?

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:20.562
- Mmm.

00:07:20.562 --> 00:07:21.395
- Still needs progress.

00:07:21.395 --> 00:07:22.228
- Mm.

00:07:22.228 --> 00:07:23.061
- We need a lot of help.

00:07:23.061 --> 00:07:23.894
- Yeah.

00:07:23.894 --> 00:07:24.881
- There's a lot of help.

00:07:24.881 --> 00:07:25.714
- Yeah.

00:07:25.714 --> 00:07:26.547
- I feel like we're in a time

00:07:26.547 --> 00:07:28.982
when we talk a lot about
diversity and inclusion

00:07:28.982 --> 00:07:31.061
and what we're gonna do to change things,

00:07:31.061 --> 00:07:32.802
but the action. We need action.

00:07:32.802 --> 00:07:33.781
- Seeing it actually implemented.

00:07:33.781 --> 00:07:34.775
- Yes, we need action.

00:07:34.775 --> 00:07:35.608
- We need more-

00:07:35.608 --> 00:07:36.441
- Yeah.

00:07:36.441 --> 00:07:38.519
- people of color in
boardrooms in the senior seats.

00:07:38.519 --> 00:07:39.352
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:39.352 --> 00:07:40.919
- Not just in middle management.
So that's what I'm trying.

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:43.428
- [Kelly] I agree. I love that.

00:07:43.428 --> 00:07:44.386
- [Bozoma] Yeah.

00:07:44.386 --> 00:07:45.219
- [Kelly] Fabulous.

00:07:45.219 --> 00:07:46.560
- I mean.
- I mean.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:47.718
- But don't we make the boardrooms

00:07:47.718 --> 00:07:48.855
just that much more fabulous?

00:07:48.855 --> 00:07:49.688
I mean, look at it.

00:07:49.688 --> 00:07:50.521
Like, it's just-

00:07:50.521 --> 00:07:51.661
- I would go to that job.

00:07:51.661 --> 00:07:54.253
I'm like, I'd wanna go to that meeting.

00:07:54.253 --> 00:07:55.682
I'm like, what are we talking about?

00:07:55.682 --> 00:07:56.556
So let's talk about the book.

00:07:56.556 --> 00:07:57.389
- Yeah.

00:07:57.389 --> 00:07:58.936
- So it's called "The Urgent Life".

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:00.968
And you open up about your life a lot,

00:08:00.968 --> 00:08:03.053
which I think is incredibly brave

00:08:03.053 --> 00:08:04.546
'cause it's hard to do that.

00:08:04.546 --> 00:08:05.464
I don't know why I would do that.

00:08:05.464 --> 00:08:07.596
So why did you wanna write it now?

00:08:07.596 --> 00:08:08.984
Why did you feel like it was important?

00:08:08.984 --> 00:08:12.034
- You know, there's, along my
career, as you could imagine,

00:08:12.034 --> 00:08:13.720
people have told me to
write the marketing book.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:14.553
You know that-

00:08:14.553 --> 00:08:15.386
- Yeah.

00:08:15.386 --> 00:08:16.695
- the "How To Be The
Marketing Executive" book.

00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:17.528
- Yeah.

00:08:17.528 --> 00:08:21.353
- But I have also found
that we have been taught

00:08:21.353 --> 00:08:23.127
to be like two different people, right?

00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:24.130
You have the personal side

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:25.336
and you have the professional side.

00:08:25.336 --> 00:08:26.169
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:27.002
- But that's actually not real.

00:08:27.002 --> 00:08:27.835
That's not a true thing.

00:08:27.835 --> 00:08:28.668
You're one person, right?

00:08:28.668 --> 00:08:29.501
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:29.501 --> 00:08:31.000
- And it's very, very difficult to show up

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.908
as your whole self in a job

00:08:32.908 --> 00:08:34.892
if you're leaving some of it on the side.

00:08:34.892 --> 00:08:37.392
You know, especially given
the fact that we've all

00:08:37.392 --> 00:08:40.237
been through so much, you know,
everybody goes through loss.

00:08:40.237 --> 00:08:41.314
Everybody goes through trauma.

00:08:41.314 --> 00:08:43.896
Sometimes it's not maybe
what I have been through,

00:08:43.896 --> 00:08:44.952
but you've been through something.

00:08:44.952 --> 00:08:45.785
- Different challenges.

00:08:45.785 --> 00:08:46.872
- And how to show for work like that.

00:08:46.872 --> 00:08:48.930
- You know, are you supposed
to leave it outside?

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.584
How are you supposed to manage?

00:08:50.584 --> 00:08:52.504
You know, and so part of the reason

00:08:52.504 --> 00:08:53.624
I want to write the book right now

00:08:53.624 --> 00:08:55.191
is because of what we've all,

00:08:55.191 --> 00:08:56.024
as the whole global world,

00:08:56.024 --> 00:08:57.440
been through in the last three years.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:58.978
We've all suffered a lot, you know?

00:08:58.978 --> 00:09:00.011
And how did you show up?

00:09:00.011 --> 00:09:03.673
Not just at work, but, like,
in your community at school,

00:09:03.673 --> 00:09:04.993
like in the different areas that we're in

00:09:04.993 --> 00:09:07.341
and that perhaps we can be more honest

00:09:07.341 --> 00:09:08.664
about what we're going through,

00:09:08.664 --> 00:09:10.338
and therefore be able to
help each other some more.

00:09:10.338 --> 00:09:11.736
- [Kelly] Because chances
are everybody else

00:09:11.736 --> 00:09:14.157
in that room has got their
own different version-

00:09:14.157 --> 00:09:14.990
- [Bozoma] Yes.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.034
- of your private health.

00:09:16.034 --> 00:09:17.090
- [Bozoma] Okay, exactly.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:18.636
- [Kelly] Everybody's
got something going on.

00:09:18.636 --> 00:09:19.469
I agree with that as well.

00:09:19.469 --> 00:09:21.101
It's hard to compartmentalize.

00:09:21.101 --> 00:09:22.391
That was actually a big thing for me.

00:09:22.391 --> 00:09:23.618
Like, even just going through divorce

00:09:23.618 --> 00:09:24.514
and, like, doing this job

00:09:24.514 --> 00:09:26.029
and showing up and smiling

00:09:26.029 --> 00:09:27.725
and, like, you know, you're
supposed to do something,

00:09:27.725 --> 00:09:29.538
but it's, like, you're, I'm a human.

00:09:29.538 --> 00:09:31.117
Like, you know, and, like, yeah,

00:09:31.117 --> 00:09:32.920
I'm glad the show isn't live,

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.061
but it's a hard thing to ask people.

00:09:36.061 --> 00:09:38.285
It's a hard thing to ask of
people without, like, saying,

00:09:38.285 --> 00:09:39.448
well, no, you're allowed to be human-

00:09:39.448 --> 00:09:40.281
- [Bozoma] Right.

00:09:40.281 --> 00:09:41.208
- in a moment.

00:09:41.208 --> 00:09:42.041
- [Kelly] Or in all the moments.

